{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he also comes toward you.</b> It is possible to interpret <b>וגם</b> ('and also') as an inclusion — to include the sar (angelic prince) of Esav, who also comes toward him to do battle with him; and this is the subject of the passage where it is written, ״ויאבק איש עמו״ ('and a man wrestled with him'). This also strengthens what I wrote above concerning the matter of the angels — for how could human beings have known of the matter of the angel who came to wrestle with him? And this is the reason for the doubled expression, ויירא ויצר ('and he was afraid and he was distressed'), for the fear was because of Esav and the distress was because of the sar of Esav." "<b>And four hundred men with him.</b> They are prepared for battle. And it does not say אתו ('with him,' with the particle את), for that expression is used of the righteous; as in the case of Yosef (Genesis 39:23): ״באשר ה׳ אתו״ ('because HaShem was with him'). And Scripture used the expression עמו ('with him') in the manner of what is written (II Chronicles 32:8): ״עמו זרוע בשר ועמנו ה׳ אלהינו לעזרנו״ ('with him is the arm of flesh, and with us is HaShem our Elohim to help us')."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:04.010002269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}